<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xi'an Incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> Incident was the detention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> in Xi'an in December 1936 by his own generals Zhang Xueliang and Yang Hucheng, who wanted him to stop fighting the Communists and resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After about two weeks of negotiations, in which the Communists took part, Chiang was released. The incident led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a> of Nationalists and Communists against Japan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xian-incident.jpg" alt="Xi'an Incident" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Xueliang,_Yang_Hucheng_and_Chiang_Kai-shek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and the Nationalists had been at war since 1927. After the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> ordered General Zhang Xueliang not to resist and adopted the policy of first internal pacification, then external resistance. In October 1935 the Communists reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, having lost over 90 percent of their numbers. They were hemmed in by the Northeastern Army under Zhang, 130,000 strong, and the Northwestern Army under Yang Hucheng, 40,000 strong, both based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. Secret talks between the two parties in 1936 stalled, while the Communists forged a covert alliance with both armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chiang flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> on 4 December 1936 to prepare another assault on the Communists. On 9 December he threatened Zhang and Yang with removal and the reassignment of their forces to distant southern provinces. The historian Rana Mitter suggests that this may have confirmed Zhang's intention to act. On 12 December they seized Chiang and demanded an end to the civil war and an alliance with the Communists against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Mao Zedong at first approved putting Chiang on trial.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, fearing a Japanese plot to weaken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, overruled the Communists and ordered a peaceful resolution. General He Yingqin nearly launched an assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> before Soong Mei-ling and T. V. Soong prevailed in favour of negotiation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>, Qin Bangxian and Ye Jianying joined the talks, and Zhou persuaded Yang's faction and the young officers who wanted to execute Chiang to negotiate his release. Chiang conceded to a ceasefire, and on 25 December Zhang released him and flew with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Chiang was greeted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> by cheering crowds of over 400,000 people and immediately had Zhang arrested; Zhang remained under house arrest until 1990. Yang was arrested in 1937 and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> shortly before the city fell to the Communists in 1949. The final pieces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a> were agreed in late September 1937, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> had begun.</p><p>The incident is the subject of often partisan histories. To the Nationalists it was a lost opportunity to destroy the Communists. In the Communist account the party was the country's saviour, committed to resistance while Chiang obstinately put the civil war first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> called Zhang Xueliang a hero of the nation, and Zhang said in the 1990s that he had no regrets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xi'an Incident” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The campaign grew after the <b>Xi'an Incident</b> of December 1936 eased Nationalist pressure on the left.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li></ul></section>
